rly young  people was more than enough motivation  and that's what i'm excited about the  young people that get to see it because  it's coming at such a very dicey time in  our in our in our country where there's  a lot of mental health there's a lot of  anguish there's a lot of sadness and i  think there are so many messages and so  many  layers to this movie absolutely i mean  the two that always really stand out to  me particularly for right now the first  is probably the more obvious which is  you know isolation and loneliness are  more universal than ever particularly  for these young people who have been  stuck at home and who have missed the  social experience of school and who are  feeling trapped in their own minds and  in their own you know mental health  struggles and i think the movie is a  really beautiful reminder of our shared  humanity and the fact that we all want  to be connected and that regardless of  how we present we're all going through  something and i think that the other is  particularly in this age of you know  cancer culture and kind of very harsh  criticism and kind of finite criticism i  think it's a beautiful thing for young  people to see a character who makes a  big mistake who does something wrong to  not only understand where that's coming  from but also to watch someone like that  heal and find forgiveness from himself  and from others and move forward and  that you know we all by virtue of being  human beings are going to make  mistakes to know that he's not a hero  here no no it's you know he's a  complicated character and i think that  that's the kind of character that  represents humanity you know none of us  are heroes doing good things all the  time and yeah there is a duality in your  character that you capture there's a  strength and then there's also a  fragility um so one i just want to say i  commend you on that because that is all  of us whether we want to admit it or not  but going back to the broadway hit i i i  was 