  wearing these gray new balance and  something about those shoes felt like  him to me so the actual pair that i wore  in all three productions is also in the  film because i've carried them with me  and they've sort of been my little good  luck charm so they they now sit very  ripped up on my shelf  so  they're  i didn't even notice the shoes i noticed  the way you mold your body and the way  you twist and turned and you captured a  high school angst so beautifully thank  you do you see yourself in evan hansen  at all i do i think you know we we both  share you know struggles with anxiety  and i think we both are pretty fearful  like non-confrontational people and i  think  sometimes much like evan i can shy away  from more painful awkward difficult  conversations that can lead to deeper  connection and i feel like playing him  has and seeing what he goes through and  what could have been avoided if he was  willing to do the to to have those  conversations it encourages me to push  myself off of that cliff as much as  possible and plunge into those  situations because i know how meaningful  they can be i struggled with your  character though ben because i knew what  he did was wrong but on the other hand i  it gave the family  such hope and such joy that i almost  didn't want them to know the truth but i  knew the truth had to come out so i  found myself sitting there struggling as  i watched you i think that's what's  beautiful about the piece is that it's  it's ambiguous and it forces you to look  at yourself and what you might do in  that situation and i think there's not a  person who couldn't understand why  looking at this grieving mother you  wouldn't want to give her exactly what  she wants and seeing what evan is going  through and how alone he feels how  wonderful it would be for him to have an  acceptance with this family and have  somewhere to belong and it doesn't  excuse it and it doesn't condone it but  it it allows us to understand the  humanity of 